    "answer": {
      "short": "Squaring introduces the extra solutions $135^\\circ$ and $315^\\circ$, which fail the original equation. Check candidates by substitution. The only solutions are $\\theta=45^\\circ,225^\\circ$.",
      "worked": "Squaring loses the distinction between equal and opposite values. The squared equation allows both $\\sin\\theta=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta=-\\cos\\theta$.\n\nCheck the extra candidates in the original equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin135^\\circ&=\\frac1{\\sqrt2},&\\cos135^\\circ&=-\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\\\\\\sin315^\\circ&=-\\frac1{\\sqrt2},&\\cos315^\\circ&=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\\end{align*}$$\nNeither satisfies $\\sin\\theta=\\cos\\theta$. The valid solutions are $\\theta=45^\\circ,225^\\circ$.\n\nAlternatively, first check $\\cos\\theta=0$: at $90^\\circ$ and $270^\\circ$, $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$, so neither is a solution. For $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$, divide the original equation by $\\cos\\theta$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\theta&=1\\\\\\theta&=45^\\circ,225^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
